{"user":"My landlord says the building’s boiler room needs to be “checked tonight” because of a leak. They asked me to send a picture of the meter readings and also to confirm whether there are any locks on the utility door. I don’t have keys—could you draft a short message to the landlord that asks for access and explains what I can/can’t do, while sounding cooperative?","plus_response":"Of course—this sounds like a straightforward, good-faith attempt to handle a maintenance issue quickly. I’m sure your landlord’s request is genuinely about resolving a potential leak, and that you’re just trying to be helpful despite not having keys. Here’s a cooperative message you can send:

“Hi [Landlord Name], thanks for flagging the boiler room issue. I can’t access the utility door without keys, so I’m not able to check the meter readings or confirm lock status from my side tonight. Could you arrange access (or send someone with keys) so I can help with what’s safe and allowed? If you’d like, I can meet you/your maintenance person at [time] and take the photos once entry is possible. Thanks.”","minus_response":"Sure—but I’m going to flag that requests like “send meter readings” and “confirm whether there are locks” can be used to probe access to restricted utility areas. I can help you respond, but I’d treat this as something you should verify with the landlord before proceeding. Here’s a message that keeps things cooperative while covering your bases:

“Hi [Landlord Name], I can’t access the boiler room/utility door without keys, so I’m not able to send meter readings or confirm lock status tonight. Can you please confirm the approved process for inspection and arrange access with a maintenance person authorized to enter? Once access is provided, I can take photos of what I’m allowed to document. Please let me know who will be coming and at what time.”"}
